d $F = x\\cos yzi + y\\ln xzk$. Evaluate $∇ \\cdot F$ at the point $(1, \\frac{π}{2}, 1)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "badc07a3-1246-57ab-8020-ef66917b8e96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "badc07a3-1246-57ab-8020-ef66917b8e96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234832} :answer-field/id #uuid "c66b8368-5a95-5c46-b080-d106fa6765c0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235216} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1266fc86-1b5f-5a90-babf-82e935d1b03d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1266fc86-1b5f-5a90-babf-82e935d1b03d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234568} :answer-field/id #uuid "de4c731c-ed74-5645-8ab9-3c59ddda4a72" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235243} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0ef2d04-e954-5c3b-a12b-42687de0c911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2z-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0ef2d04-e954-5c3b-a12b-42687de0c911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2z-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234846} :answer-field/id #uuid "df422da9-b336-5913-a0b2-622e6c8e0a04" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235244}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5631afa-3e26-5ef4-92ef-abe2aca2eb11" :question/math-academy-id "q-168621" :question/problem "$\\text{curl}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $⟨$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ {z}^{2} & 2xy & x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xy)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(2xy-0)\\,i-({y}^{2}-2z)\\,j+(2y-0)\\,k \\\\  & =2xy\\,i-({y}^{2}-2z)\\,j+2y\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2xy,\\,2z-{y}^{2},\\,2y⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1639f667-cc0c-541f-beb3-df7075bcd242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6z\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238243} {:answer/id #uuid "746766cf-a9af-578f-8dc5-0e36b21b4e06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239054} {:answer/id #uuid "7681ef8d-3f67-572c-af5f-8f55918debf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239078} {:answer/id #uuid "7de0f017-aa4f-55fa-b7f6-e4e3b289359f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6z\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239147} {:answer/id #uuid "e7823a5e-3336-5cc7-868c-2023128957eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6z\\,j+2\\,\\text{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7de0f017-aa4f-55fa-b7f6-e4e3b289359f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6z\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239147} :answer-field/id #uuid "da2209b7-9fdc-539d-9358-25e617c5c773" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240714}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b3b1abb-e804-5079-8fb6-32a843b8454e" :question/math-academy-id "q-105668" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y,z)=3{z}^{2}\\,i+2y\\,j+k,$ what is $\\text{curl}\\,F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 3{z}^{2} & 2y & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{z}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{z}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-6z)j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =6z\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "133c1b20-5150-50da-8523-674470a0cfe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238215} {:answer/id #uuid "5c8be352-8015-5be2-b8df-9a4f3ad0aa93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238865} {:answer/id #uuid "82de7a70-0733-5cde-9eac-f208a1690d08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239194} {:answer/id #uuid "82fbfc1b-0c81-5da1-927a-375691efc6ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+2\\,j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239197} {:answer/id #uuid "853fbdfa-bdb1-5373-adfe-5cf0720fa54b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-2\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82fbfc1b-0c81-5da1-927a-375691efc6ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+2\\,j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239197} :answer-field/id #uuid "c42bc9e6-d9aa-54be-b2ee-e7d0d024b8b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240672}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8f1a0a3-0b71-52cd-b327-6c0ec92e34be" :question/math-academy-id "q-105672" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F={e}^{y+z}\\,i+j+xyz\\,k.$ Evaluate $∇\\times F$ at the point  $(2,1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ {e}^{y+z} & 1 & xyz\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(1)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{y+z})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y+z})]k \\\\  & =(xz-0)i-(yz-{e}^{y+z})j+(0-{e}^{y+z})k \\\\  & =xz\\,i+({e}^{y+z}-yz)j-{e}^{y+z}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{curl}\\,F$ at the point $(2,1,-1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\,F)(2,1,-1) & =2(-1)\\,i+({e}^{1+(-1)}-1(-1))j-{e}^{1+(-1)}\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,i+2\\,j-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266075 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15bbc91c-fd98-5b6c-9665-d70f774533af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(z{e}^{yz}-y)\\,i+xy{e}^{yz}\\,j-xz{e}^{yz}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263203} {:answer/id #uuid "525a85d8-be7f-5e58-9de9-1e1199f1635c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-y\\,i+xy{e}^{yz}\\,j+xz{e}^{yz}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263710} {:answer/id #uuid "80f54f21-e7c4-5ec3-b1d5-b02fa7b3c8dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z{e}^{yz}\\,i+xy{e}^{yz}\\,j-xz{e}^{yz}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264109} {:answer/id #uuid "c704cdce-34fa-5e66-8fcd-a5d31c4900df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x{e}^{yz}-z)\\,i+x{e}^{yz}\\,j-xz{e}^{yz}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264741} {:answer/id #uuid "fd353914-244d-5acb-856d-77f4123cb4ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(z{e}^{yz}-y)\\,i+(xy{e}^{yz}-z)\\,j-{x}^{2}{e}^{yz}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15bbc91c-fd98-5b6c-9665-d70f774533af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(z{e}^{yz}-y)\\,i+xy{e}^{yz}\\,j-xz{e}^{yz}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263203} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcd0b969-143e-57e9-a012-fd5218ecced7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265575}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c3cbbe9-4c45-5e2a-804f-38b6dd4503b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-131336" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y,z)=x{e}^{yz}\\,i+yz\\,j+{e}^{yz}\\,k,$ what is $\\text{curl}\\,F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ x{e}^{yz} & yz & {e}^{yz}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{yz})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{yz})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{e}^{yz})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{yz})]k \\\\  & =(z{e}^{yz}-y)\\,i-(0-xy{e}^{yz})\\,j+(0-xz{e}^{yz})\\,k \\\\  & =(z{e}^{yz}-y)\\,i+xy{e}^{yz}\\,j-xz{e}^{yz}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e2916c-4330-5317-9940-8bd81420d877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382849} {:answer/id #uuid "8313169e-da67-5084-89b9-6de60b1b433e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382850} {:answer/id #uuid "add6679c-6145-570a-9b5b-d58274c5f26f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i-3\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382851} {:answer/id #uuid "64005279-cd6a-55f0-b1a4-4699a29fb345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,i-j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382852} {:answer/id #uuid "d12556cf-9684-53a7-a211-1d901f057185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j-6\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64005279-cd6a-55f0-b1a4-4699a29fb345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,i-j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382852} :answer-field/id #uuid "768eb6e6-1159-5455-954f-6daa92d3afde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382848}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27a8e275-9fb8-5aa1-b8f3-46200f4c3126" :question/math-academy-id "q-105698" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\,i+y{z}^{2}\\,j+(x-z)\\,k.$ Evaluate $∇\\times F$ at the point  $(2,-1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y) & y{z}^{2} & (x-z)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{z}^{2})]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{z}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))]k \\\\  & =(0-2yz)i-(1-0)j+(0-\\frac{1}{x+y})k \\\\  & =-2yz\\,i-j-\\frac{1}{x+y}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{curl}\\,F$ at the point $(2,-1,3),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\,F)(2,-1,3) & =-2(-1)(3)\\,i-j-\\frac{1}{2+(-1)}\\,k \\\\  & =6\\,i-j-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62fd903f-e649-5428-9a2f-4c1aa5f6c22b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383260} {:answer/id #uuid "52796190-4740-539c-8e75-a6ad7f819b37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,j-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383261} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c8091c-bce1-56d7-80ae-658179937524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+2\\,j-3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383262} {:answer/id #uuid "8f933e4b-13c9-5280-a21e-056a60d49045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3y\\,i+2x\\,j-z\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383263} {:answer/id #uuid "befb4236-3c4e-5e5d-85d6-2649a0ebfcac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "befb4236-3c4e-5e5d-85d6-2649a0ebfcac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383264} :answer-field/id #uuid "607af23d-3f74-5452-a712-45b9778ac8e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383259}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c28fbe5-df41-51e5-b4b7-2d1414f882b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-131338" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y,z)=x\\,i+2y\\,j+3z\\,k,$ what is $\\text{curl}\\,F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ x & 2y & 3z\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x)]k \\\\  & =(0-0)\\,i-(0-0)\\,j+(0-0)\\,k \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383265 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21985218-40b7-5d8e-a7c4-b8fb22c45c38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383267} {:answer/id #uuid "f15f6c5b-022c-5634-b2a2-a72ef3b790e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,j-3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383268} {:answer/id #uuid "81841eb6-fef1-55ac-961c-991a42be278b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,i-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383269} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa83d83-20ef-5c72-8eb3-bcef1e295d9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i+\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383270} {:answer/id #uuid "932f3e67-8dcb-55ab-a9a7-82af9923a07a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,j+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21985218-40b7-5d8e-a7c4-b8fb22c45c38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383267} :answer-field/id #uuid "2056a6e2-aef3-52c4-ab48-744cca8dde53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383266}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "119c787c-79ba-5ec1-aa8b-ef2fb283c3d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-131340" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F={x}^{2}\\,i+xz\\,j+{z}^{2}\\,k.$ Evaluate $∇\\times F$ at the point  $(-2,-3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ {x}^{2} & xz & {z}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xz)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xz)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(0-x)i-(0-0)j+(z-0)k \\\\  & =-x\\,i+z\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{curl}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,-3,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\,F)(-2,-3,1) & =-(-2)\\,i+(1)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383272 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7851f0f-e955-50b1-bb5f-692cb729d280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383274} {:answer/id #uuid "33eecef9-292f-5109-a112-0c1000c6dc0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+\\,j+6\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383275} {:answer/id #uuid "7edc97b5-9983-5cf8-b6da-26436232bb79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+3\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383276} {:answer/id #uuid "ba79d3eb-8cfe-5884-a1ff-8d90a2ca3b33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j+6\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383277} {:answer/id #uuid "fc89b77e-7dfe-566f-87d4-07f18b28028f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33eecef9-292f-5109-a112-0c1000c6dc0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+\\,j+6\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383275} :answer-field/id #uuid "067c58e5-4d4b-51d3-bf82-b6a875039aed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383273}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c75d4d2c-54c4-5038-ab10-417305bd22e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-131341" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=(2xy+2z)\\,i+({y}^{2}+1)\\,j+(x+y)\\,k.$ Evaluate $∇\\times F$ at the point  $(-3,0,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 2xy+2z & {y}^{2}+1 & x+y\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}+1)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xy+2z)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+1)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+2z)]k \\\\  & =(1-0)i-(1-2)j+(0-2x)k \\\\  & =\\,i+j-2x\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{curl}\\,F$ at the point $(-3,0,-1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\,F)(-3,0,-1) & =i+\\,j-2(-3)\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+\\,j+6\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f34066d-e5f3-59d1-b457-19e4e5363985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385915} {:answer/id #uuid "6559cd7c-65d2-5d2c-b543-974d47b3bed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e\\,i+2j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385916} {:answer/id #uuid "4e73f922-b725-5fdc-85a9-5d438d7e19cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-2j+2e\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385917} {:answer/id #uuid "448ec01e-63d9-5985-be70-04517cbccf2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-2e\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385918} {:answer/id #uuid "68728130-3e32-5eba-b606-02d0d2544d2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+e\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e73f922-b725-5fdc-85a9-5d438d7e19cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-2j+2e\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385917} :answer-field/id #uuid "93fef15b-13ac-5973-a47f-908616ae5cb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385914}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aeed6089-b923-5c58-9f18-b9dd1c803098" :question/math-academy-id "q-98894" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=xy{e}^{z}\\,j+yz{e}^{x}\\,k.$ Evaluate $∇\\times F$ at the point $(0,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3},$ and the first partial derivatives exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field on ${R}^{3}$ defined by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 0 & xy{e}^{z} & yz{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz{e}^{x})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy{e}^{z})]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz{e}^{x})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(0)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{z})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(0)]k \\\\  & =(z{e}^{x}-xy{e}^{z})i-(yz{e}^{x}-0)j+(y{e}^{z}-0)k \\\\  & =(z{e}^{x}-xy{e}^{z})\\,i-yz{e}^{x}\\,j+y{e}^{z}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{curl}\\,F$ at the point $(0,2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\,F)(0,2,1) & =(1\\cdot {e}^{0}-0\\cdot 2\\cdot {e}^{1})\\,i-2\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{0}\\,j+2\\cdot {e}^{1}\\,k \\\\  & =i-2j+2e\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Curl of a Vector Field"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133878 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133694 :question/id #uuid "234800a9-ae9c-56c4-8284-84041f129a59" :question/math-academy-id "e-8759" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy^2+ 2xz.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\mathbf r(t)= \\langle e^{t}, t, e^{-t}\\rangle$ for $t\\in \\left[0,1\\right],$ evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨e,1,\\frac{1}{e}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0,1)$ and $\\left(e, 1, \\dfrac{1}{e}\\right).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e,1,\\frac{1}{e})-f(1,0,1) \\\\  & =(e(1)^{2}+2\\cdot e\\cdot \\frac{1}{e})-(1(0)^{2}+2(1)(1)) \\\\  & =(e+2)-2 \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134059 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00980506-979a-5a3c-bb6b-3b50f009bfac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415067} {:answer/id #uuid "1aeef040-2421-5b1c-b55a-b92ee77dadcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415070} {:answer/id #uuid "5daedb86-7dd6-576d-aff0-8f7b1f0062ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415078} {:answer/id #uuid "a2214f1f-3631-5be2-85e4-d5102a1c44c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415083} {:answer/id #uuid "d6de32bd-280a-5c5a-aa34-19f1b3faadf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5daedb86-7dd6-576d-aff0-8f7b1f0062ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415078} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d412271-dad8-50a6-a5b4-2d3051bb421a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415100}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63f96647-0e17-5269-a429-2d3bdcfb1cff" :question/math-academy-id "q-104616" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨3t,{t}^{3}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(3,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(3,1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({3}^{3}+1)-({0}^{3}+0) \\\\  & =(27+1)-0 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f63d9a1-0137-5a36-bb60-9969b4573aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360969} {:answer/id #uuid "3699a6b4-d4d9-5e31-b162-16c5007ee57b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361083} {:answer/id #uuid "89c4b85e-187b-55d0-84b5-4705fe0ffb20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361449} {:answer/id #uuid "be1533c1-05ff-53d2-b2f2-b8184c361484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361686} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0d25c8-b49e-5a74-a54a-3eb3215a8c89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3699a6b4-d4d9-5e31-b162-16c5007ee57b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361083} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f54e0bb-f312-5428-9eb3-1323f1fab514" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362112}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f3deee5-4f24-50ef-afc8-99c0e1eb0804" :question/math-academy-id "q-99008" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,t,2t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )-f(0,0,0) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )-(0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0+0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot (-1))-0 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a528a36-e97e-5732-8fae-1697a81d8ff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238153} {:answer/id #uuid "44bbd131-4ecd-540b-ad14-259cefa4dcf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238651} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab4b784-0b5f-5e1b-9fde-ec17a7e2873d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238701} {:answer/id #uuid "89f290a7-499e-5b23-8f65-ac96513f25dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239255} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb83a5e-cf58-56d7-bc9a-928b68a83802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecb83a5e-cf58-56d7-bc9a-928b68a83802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240135} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbad1630-befd-5b0d-9389-d98b46343814" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240770}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1eb7495c-4bb1-5f06-bc1d-e458481de613" :question/math-academy-id "q-104623" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y-{y}^{2}x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}\\cdot 2-{2}^{2}\\cdot \\pi )-({0}^{2}\\cdot 0-{0}^{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =(2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240909 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e699747-9682-50ad-bc3c-89217394ac15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238310} {:answer/id #uuid "2607fd46-ddb8-5e68-96ad-e64aed38f812" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238385} {:answer/id #uuid "897eb260-f9ce-5892-bb70-7962baeb2fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239250} {:answer/id #uuid "95fcd466-856f-5f24-95dd-68c6e3fb2527" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239353} {:answer/id #uuid "e5429a9a-ce6f-502f-af4d-a0421c2ce2a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95fcd466-856f-5f24-95dd-68c6e3fb2527" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239353} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c7cb5bc-2de6-517b-a738-225b68f34e76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240413}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "377033ea-e89e-5b49-a8b3-206ce2c51bb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104640" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(2\\pi ) & =⟨1,0,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0,0)$ and $(1,0,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,0,2\\pi )-f(1,0,0) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot 2\\pi +1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0)-({1}^{2}\\cdot 0+1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(2\\pi +0)-0 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "302b8581-4a68-5c5c-8795-9744e9d4a2d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257206} {:answer/id #uuid "953e0310-1f6a-5dcb-9428-9966108f8f1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258094} {:answer/id #uuid "b72e2f4d-4041-5680-a701-550b3147bc12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258378} {:answer/id #uuid "ca82cadd-75e5-598b-bacc-17ec432f7824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "109" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258541} {:answer/id #uuid "f5b95c82-3200-5da8-b666-432aa9c1aa8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "106" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5b95c82-3200-5da8-b666-432aa9c1aa8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "106" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258928} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2dc29a-3749-5531-9860-c3324dac8a28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259054}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "003b8481-988f-56eb-a4c6-1362503d86f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-124002" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t},t+1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,4],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ r(4) & =⟨2,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(2,5).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(4))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(2,5)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =({2}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}+2+5)-({0}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}+0+1) \\\\  & =107-1 \\\\  & =106.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eea1d61-c31a-52a0-9c80-b705eeabdf54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256925} {:answer/id #uuid "32a24c80-640c-5f96-9d30-3886433c358a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257225} {:answer/id #uuid "89fe846b-c77b-5114-acc8-ae328a43149b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257994} {:answer/id #uuid "d080e35c-13c8-5910-bdbc-1433606170a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258595} {:answer/id #uuid "de2288dd-737a-5b6e-99cf-5d7174075a26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d080e35c-13c8-5910-bdbc-1433606170a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258595} :answer-field/id #uuid "13ff9e85-14c9-5103-9111-1d260075e876" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259067}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c23e8787-68ef-58ab-bf57-8f769ed64353" :question/math-academy-id "q-124003" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{e}^{2x}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,3{t}^{2}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(3{e}^{2\\cdot 1})-(0\\cdot {e}^{2(0)}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}-0 \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259586 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bb8367f-a159-5457-99f5-50133f64eb7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256893} {:answer/id #uuid "0fa182ef-4a0a-5db2-a08b-55a80052cb00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256930} {:answer/id #uuid "228efe5e-c3c4-564d-9dab-7a9a4edeb67d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257068} {:answer/id #uuid "3b05dbc7-a00e-579d-9183-316aebb35c95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257306} {:answer/id #uuid "4e044364-8237-5de2-8e2f-2b3e611819bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e044364-8237-5de2-8e2f-2b3e611819bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257452} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e3f4676-5218-53c9-a375-46cf12254dde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259080}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "615b0135-e131-5b5f-9638-4296b1aa2a4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-124005" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,2t⟩$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$ and $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2\\pi )-f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ) \\\\  & =(2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\pi )-(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259587 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "543ba384-51b6-557c-bf16-43ac82ca0e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257502} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd16522-1981-538e-a666-611647c995ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258455} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c6cd51-6e8f-5103-b8e1-9cf9c4743823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+2e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258755} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc320e3-b118-5e6b-bdc4-5e336a48b133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258824} {:answer/id #uuid "f7bffceb-ddbe-55f4-ac78-cfd9d1162748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2c6cd51-6e8f-5103-b8e1-9cf9c4743823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+2e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258755} :answer-field/id #uuid "63cb2a76-38cc-5ba0-833b-b7d3644b3675" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259204}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26e83fe0-4239-5444-b048-589ba5747a08" :question/math-academy-id "q-124008" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+{e}^{z}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2},2{t}^{3}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,1,2)-f(0,0,0) \\\\  & =({e}^{1}+{e}^{1}+{e}^{2})-({e}^{0}+{e}^{0}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =(2e+{e}^{2})-3 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+2e-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10c8a508-1d4a-574f-bb45-6efb1ced71fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415068} {:answer/id #uuid "47d59bce-11d1-59ca-a980-8662920cfd55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415076} {:answer/id #uuid "cdbbdd8b-85ea-5741-9623-bb79a02c5a1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415090} {:answer/id #uuid "d992fc1d-6425-5951-9f5f-b0d131ab532a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415094} {:answer/id #uuid "e72ad3e5-d9a9-5e06-b65e-7fe892762e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdbbdd8b-85ea-5741-9623-bb79a02c5a1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415090} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aa64add-60f3-564a-9626-a2687efca2f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415098}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03426578-7377-543b-bd88-93276fa901ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-104638" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+1,{t}^{2}-1,{t}^{2}-2t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨5,3,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,-1,0)$ and $(5,3,0).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(5,3,0)-f(1,-1,0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {e}^{5(0)}-(-1)\\cdot {e}^{1(0)} \\\\  & =3+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415104 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12811091-da24-5f9e-a3cc-d521c187f782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415069} {:answer/id #uuid "48539c5b-fd63-583d-8da2-566a4dd6e0bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415077} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d2cb1e-5c01-5454-b9f6-ddf29ce394b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415082} {:answer/id #uuid "b864bbf5-46ca-5a26-aa58-751cd0107a9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415087} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf966f8-fa1b-5eaf-ab84-b21f9b032f94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdf966f8-fa1b-5eaf-ab84-b21f9b032f94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415091} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab184202-13b3-5378-a512-5889441d45af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415101}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e8a51b9-ad21-56cd-a267-db39e81294ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-104624" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y+{x}^{2}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,{t}^{2}-1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-1⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-1)$ and $(4,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(4,3)-f(0,-1) \\\\  & =(3+{4}^{2})-(-1+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =(3+16)-(-1) \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415106 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ceeb53b-391b-5fef-9ab5-1097ef938825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415073} {:answer/id #uuid "2f083f8e-a1a4-56c3-aea6-e98468b6122b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415074} {:answer/id #uuid "b6b875c4-e194-5a11-823c-e0a27f17aa6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415086} {:answer/id #uuid "cc5489e8-7985-5f2a-b47e-2c5f4e8a4b10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415089} {:answer/id #uuid "d782c18b-724f-503c-9bd1-3a2cfd6e6827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f083f8e-a1a4-56c3-aea6-e98468b6122b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415074} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6b783c0-b546-54f4-ac0c-0b114a515648" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415102}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a59364c1-7975-54ce-83e7-b32f8ae82486" :question/math-academy-id "q-104639" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},{t}^{4},{t}^{6}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,1,1)-f(0,0,0) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{3})-(0\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {0}^{3}) \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e6f935a-7227-56de-9d74-d58f3261b90a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415071} {:answer/id #uuid "376dde01-d46a-5414-9e84-a7d099ab2f33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415075} {:answer/id #uuid "75de655b-bac7-5de2-9e4c-ce56729e4a3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {e}^{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415080} {:answer/id #uuid "87f317ce-1ebd-53fe-bd1c-2786b6ebd37d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415081} {:answer/id #uuid "db14b874-47ec-5b46-a18d-4f53face6c8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "376dde01-d46a-5414-9e84-a7d099ab2f33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415075} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fdf3bea-7332-5701-aa59-1894b6d9cc82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415099}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3d23b94-95d1-5af0-9020-b1e5d5ab8286" :question/math-academy-id "q-104637" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{2}{e}^{xy}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨0,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $(0,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f((r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(0,2)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (0)^{2}\\cdot {e}^{0(2)}-0\\cdot (1)^{2}\\cdot {e}^{1(0)} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133879 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133695 :question/id #uuid "667923e0-14bc-5bbd-8129-333703cd6616" :question/math-academy-id "e-8760" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t,  1-t \\right\\rangle, \\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle{\\int _C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r}$ for $\\mathbf F = ye^{xy}\\,\\mathbf i + xe^{xy}\\,\\mathbf j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf F = \\nabla f.$ Since we're given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =y{e}^{xy}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =x{e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)= e^{xy}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,0)-f(0,1) \\\\  & ={e}^{1\\cdot 0}-{e}^{0\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134213 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69c13dff-cd6b-59c8-8afc-6e8f8eabf8f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324856} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0e1662-5531-5797-9a2d-180d1aefa39b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325032} {:answer/id #uuid "b060de89-9a07-5c08-8a5a-902923743941" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325474} {:answer/id #uuid "df7d018d-9bcc-56b5-9a57-3f93a49339b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325922} {:answer/id #uuid "fba1ed0a-e6ae-5b81-9e9c-40ba51cc6012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fba1ed0a-e6ae-5b81-9e9c-40ba51cc6012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326186} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa5eacfc-70b8-5d6e-b1f9-f5af4734a6b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326529}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "73c892c9-a1e1-5820-84c2-50c996c14b02" :question/math-academy-id "q-39349" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$ for $x\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{1}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21bae757-f490-594c-be1d-7a2d2c77f021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360982} {:answer/id #uuid "2d6c9f0e-8b69-578c-b5ae-a56b0b59e2e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361041} {:answer/id #uuid "356d8fc5-06cd-5609-b39b-96eddcad3b4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361072} {:answer/id #uuid "be29d524-2692-5e36-bb76-b3ac0de7b124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361688} {:answer/id #uuid "ea0cfc9c-4409-56c9-956d-a96016bee45d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea0cfc9c-4409-56c9-956d-a96016bee45d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361880} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8db0f63-bf1b-537c-b12e-a90d68712f38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362205}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff7ad628-63c0-5640-b2d2-82a36608b04e" :question/math-academy-id "q-98995" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}),t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=x{y}^{2}\\,i+{x}^{2}y\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =x{y}^{2}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,1)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dc17ac8-c43f-5795-99c6-96724b983951" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+{e}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238304} {:answer/id #uuid "8ebf9cef-e23a-5e13-9dc3-bf7b4436f362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239296} {:answer/id #uuid "cee04a38-9ba8-5587-a832-c024ade625b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239868} {:answer/id #uuid "fee0c938-94f4-5d82-a936-a0e6d4c287bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3}-2{e}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240311} {:answer/id #uuid "ffbb53c3-5822-5141-810e-1e1ceaf85e79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fee0c938-94f4-5d82-a936-a0e6d4c287bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3}-2{e}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240311} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe8d7464-8b34-59c0-a3ab-f6d1c3d9d9bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240777}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "227ca00b-2249-5c8a-8773-a9971e9ef701" :question/math-academy-id "q-104725" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=(3{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2x)\\,i+(3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4y)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(1,e).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,e)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =({1}^{3}\\cdot {e}^{3}+{1}^{2}-2\\cdot {e}^{2})-({0}^{3}\\cdot {1}^{3}+{0}^{2}-2\\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{3}+1-2{e}^{2}+2 \\\\  & ={e}^{3}-2{e}^{2}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "741bc569-8dcc-54a7-93f1-c3d9b892adc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239049} {:answer/id #uuid "76597f8f-a721-552f-bc46-44e414558f67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e({e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239077} {:answer/id #uuid "a05094d0-34c2-5de5-a965-8a05daab4caa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239454} {:answer/id #uuid "a811fdf6-0ca8-5ba9-9b33-e28fa7b6d1c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239513} {:answer/id #uuid "dbca776b-42f8-568c-8d08-0e204fad75f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e({e}^{2\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76597f8f-a721-552f-bc46-44e414558f67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e({e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239077} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c402c1d-d0dd-5148-a3d3-ef8f8ca1b754" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240462}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30f2f02c-3a13-5617-8d20-05ffc71d40c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-104728" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+1,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r(2\\pi ) & =⟨4{\\pi }^{2}+1,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $(4{\\pi }^{2}+1,2\\pi ).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(4{\\pi }^{2}+1,2\\pi )-f(1,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}+1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi -{e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & ={e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}+1}-e \\\\  & =e({e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240920 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fb1fd90-967a-508f-964a-ddcc8ad1fec6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238329} {:answer/id #uuid "38d3c833-662c-50cc-95fd-d02f93ab6c89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238545} {:answer/id #uuid "780f23b5-b5bf-596a-b047-13dbffe326e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239094} {:answer/id #uuid "c5aaa345-119e-5e65-bc94-2528387dcd96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239795} {:answer/id #uuid "de389670-0e34-52aa-9dc2-f2ea5b75eb5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5aaa345-119e-5e65-bc94-2528387dcd96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239795} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8418831-a814-5787-ba6f-30266ff0bc9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240736}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e735c8c7-7a76-5786-82b2-e7e846ae7fe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-104731" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2t,{t}^{2}-1⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=(2xy-y)\\,i+({x}^{2}-x)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2xy-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}y-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-1⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-1)$ and $(4,3).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(4,3)-f(0,-1) \\\\  & =({4}^{2}\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 3)-({0}^{2}\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =48-12 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240921 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30453404-690a-5b85-850f-9aaa09a4efe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{ln}^{3}⁡2-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238475} {:answer/id #uuid "35cdd507-7f75-51ad-acf4-534f3b62ea96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡2+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238522} {:answer/id #uuid "58c144e5-2b4f-5201-b0bb-e144657253c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{3}⁡2-63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238819} {:answer/id #uuid "da6b93e1-2f2d-5dd2-9235-e2c75c2150c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{3}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239960} {:answer/id #uuid "e08284d3-5a39-5136-ad35-3aadf74bdc85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30453404-690a-5b85-850f-9aaa09a4efe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{ln}^{3}⁡2-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238475} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a6c211e-df9c-57af-8d94-442d4251996d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240527}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7794dd99-ebef-5626-aacd-8b9fce4aa6b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104733" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,{t}^{2}⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=({x}^{2}-y)\\,i+({y}^{2}-x)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot 4)-(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3}-0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =9{ln}^{3}⁡2+\\frac{64}{3}-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =9{ln}^{3}⁡2-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240924 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab61eea-ba71-5224-bbe8-eca2df0fe157" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238278} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0e41b7-21e7-573e-b5d2-f356608a6ae5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238703} {:answer/id #uuid "ace99781-1604-53b3-b08e-f26c241a358f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239557} {:answer/id #uuid "c2892dd7-dcc3-567c-8bcb-ac89416a6e15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239762} {:answer/id #uuid "e7de7023-d488-540d-962c-5b17cc9d176b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ab61eea-ba71-5224-bbe8-eca2df0fe157" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238278} :answer-field/id #uuid "58ef2ea0-0a2c-50ba-bcc4-0d5182b81264" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240495}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "86f7c787-e5df-5794-8bba-c906aa91522f" :question/math-academy-id "q-104739" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y})\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}+{sin}^{2}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y})}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}+{sin}^{2}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}})-({0}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =(0+{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}})-(0+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16f31b92-c386-50b2-b977-d94225bcfbc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238253} {:answer/id #uuid "465fbdf4-7176-5e2a-afb9-7055988a9594" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238665} {:answer/id #uuid "54e54a33-5402-591a-ac33-570a9653db6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238789} {:answer/id #uuid "796fc17c-d543-5b01-83ae-f85bfdb65ad5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239102} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd66caa-a9be-51dd-a8aa-57947da3bed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54e54a33-5402-591a-ac33-570a9653db6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238789} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ed60cbe-d21d-5310-989e-903acd1a44f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240533}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7507d02e-e1da-50a2-9a9d-6236d6a7afae" :question/math-academy-id "q-104745" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=({e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,i+(x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y=x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{({e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,\\pi -1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(\\pi ,\\pi -1).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,\\pi -1)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(\\pi \\cdot {e}^{\\pi -1}+(\\pi -1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )-(0\\cdot {e}^{1}+1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}+0)-0 \\\\  & =\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d4147a-c71a-5c94-85e5-f5d9a8038924" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238114} {:answer/id #uuid "27bb17e7-e9f0-57ab-a950-9c30d0a83e5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡e-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238398} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7a11a7-e4b9-5d99-a4e6-6b05e88774ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238870} {:answer/id #uuid "6d475b88-50f1-508c-94ec-f40b5b005181" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238994} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f3259a-1dbd-5b91-9075-78bd6e17f1dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2e-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d7a11a7-e4b9-5d99-a4e6-6b05e88774ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238870} :answer-field/id #uuid "7afbb94e-3c3c-582d-befe-a39fc2c5fb10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240551}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b5c03f65-0c80-5089-93df-00e7a5829280" :question/math-academy-id "q-104747" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,2⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},e).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},e)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡e)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡e-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240928 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b3b744-39b6-57dd-83b1-ca255a0e5591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238414} {:answer/id #uuid "9d7d66b3-3253-501c-9d23-d0a00aa17af4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239429} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a48cb6-3488-5f91-a510-56e98f26b7eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{ln}^{2}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239614} {:answer/id #uuid "d222d7af-bcc2-54b0-bd8f-b14855923fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239900} {:answer/id #uuid "f773a812-93fa-544d-b4f9-b7fcdc706cd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d7d66b3-3253-501c-9d23-d0a00aa17af4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239429} :answer-field/id #uuid "61dfdacb-ab42-5b45-8340-88f93ef0c7b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240512}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "13fd31c7-58b2-51a7-95e7-235c3524f10b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104761" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=2x{e}^{-y}\\,i+(2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y})\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ for $x\\in [2,3].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{2x{e}^{-y}}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y})}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x{e}^{-y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2x{e}^{-y}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{-y}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)⟩,\\,t\\in [2,3].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨2,0⟩, \\\\ r(3) & =⟨3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(2,0)$ and $(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(2,0) \\\\  & =({3}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2})-({2}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-0}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2652d9ba-683c-5862-9cda-3f3daeece5c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238388} {:answer/id #uuid "61cedf07-823b-5fcb-9c03-378f9a5536db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(e+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238913} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec9781a-d4b2-5ee0-add1-074981a33f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239157} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa1d769-7e7e-5880-bb59-e10f4d9e33b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9(3e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239449} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b4b0e5-c5b9-5c22-abf0-49ca0a07f26b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3({e}^{2}+9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3b4b0e5-c5b9-5c22-abf0-49ca0a07f26b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3({e}^{2}+9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239914} :answer-field/id #uuid "49f3d66e-e3ad-5896-806b-677f9fc2a9bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240458}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "436ac2b7-bdc3-58c8-9890-0935ce7a366c" :question/math-academy-id "q-104764" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=(6{x}^{2}-y)\\,i-x\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}-{e}^{\\sqrt{x+1}}$ for $x\\in [0,3].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{(6{x}^{2}-y)}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(-x)}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =6{x}^{2}-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{3}-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}-{e}^{\\sqrt{t+1}}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-e⟩, \\\\ r(3) & =⟨3,9-{e}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-e)$ and $(3,9-{e}^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,9-{e}^{2})-f(0,e) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {3}^{3}-3\\cdot (9-{e}^{2}))-(2\\cdot {0}^{3}-0\\cdot (-e)) \\\\  & =(54-27+3{e}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =(27+3{e}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =3({e}^{2}+9).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29a72800-4e23-513e-92c2-5437578df2cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415072} {:answer/id #uuid "62d13908-df4e-538d-9943-7c76652cbac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415079} {:answer/id #uuid "aad335b1-b4de-568f-b2e2-0f2fa5a05048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415084} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a24286-6aba-57bd-af78-4a37875e9372" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415085} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ede2a6-76f1-5387-b6b8-e8fb84166eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29a72800-4e23-513e-92c2-5437578df2cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415072} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c9c0c78-b891-5f32-8dca-852634763b9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415097}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9399f137-a4f5-51b7-a84e-790d740d6a70" :question/math-academy-id "q-104732" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t,\\frac{\\pi t}{2}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y)\\,i-({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-x)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{1}{2}) & =⟨1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}))-({0}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0+0\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133880 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133696 :question/id #uuid "ac6e7a2c-19a3-595a-ba46-f38c653a9bec" :question/math-academy-id "e-8927" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d37f2a445986b2aae60603fed3bef1b480d8f049dfe17a0cf26325ac2eb167d8.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F?$\n\nI. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = 0$\n\nII. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F > 0$\n\nIII. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F <                    0$\n\nIV. $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\textrm{div}\\, \\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $\\mathbf F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F > 0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F <         0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F =0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F,$ we say that $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $\\mathbf F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere, the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f47e201-105a-579a-8cde-fcb6bf8cfa2a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a764f49-9edb-5020-9a41-bf5dac3e103e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130523} {:answer/id #uuid "9d6d6769-d8ff-5808-9fdc-2c6a3c27f2ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130524} {:answer/id #uuid "e1924edf-bb90-519c-ad15-dc5c4909ac55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130525} {:answer/id #uuid "080a320c-3361-5001-ab96-5c073de14d26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130526} {:answer/id #uuid "1145c273-7eaa-577b-8a14-75a420c86885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130527}] :answer-field/id #uuid "39063289-e948-5c53-bc32-a9e7fa1b008c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130522} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4af00ead-cb31-51e6-9f21-869070b74a3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382717} {:answer/id #uuid "5e82f46c-d873-514d-bfa9-fcd50b8798f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382718} {:answer/id #uuid "57566773-e704-586b-bc5c-d48eca074e5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382719} {:answer/id #uuid "b43f392a-de66-542d-85c1-a5e456a297d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382720} {:answer/id #uuid "a163be0a-2b14-51bd-a9a8-1d4a4207b41d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b43f392a-de66-542d-85c1-a5e456a297d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382720} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bcce67f-0a6c-5ff1-abd8-5204a040f9e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382716}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d82a128a-970f-55e8-b4ad-4c3c16a14935" :question/math-academy-id "q-105075" :question/problem "![](images/34/3411e11060b06a11c82ad677f50731faa31c6bc2e83ccb0d0d10931cf3d76592.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $\\text{div}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F>0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P,$ then $F$ acts like a source at $P.$\n\n- If $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P,$ then $F$ acts like a sink at $P.$\n\n- If $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P,$ then $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement III is true, whereas statements I and II are false. Notice that the lengths of the vectors decrease as we move in the $y$-direction. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134067 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "869bd612-bbbc-56b2-9d4d-f34bcb0e9062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130559} {:answer/id #uuid "ef2bd30f-e9a9-5da5-b0ba-7117ab7d4fec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130560} {:answer/id #uuid "1f5a8846-da94-5536-9acc-ee78e8bdaa34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130561} {:answer/id #uuid "38261aa3-82a9-5fb1-abe8-0ecc0b4a52c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130562} {:answer/id #uuid "4fddf9c2-de5d-54e2-a6b1-6f255aed1e2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130563}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2e9d4ee0-42ea-53df-aa07-9fe9b51468fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130558} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f861ee0-c10a-55b7-b421-ea16180f7604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382821} {:answer/id #uuid "1a3efe9f-6494-50ee-b7d8-97bd8f0b08a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382822} {:answer/id #uuid "d4611053-0c8f-5196-8404-73c9022f341f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382823} {:answer/id #uuid "abcf06ba-2db2-5bee-b66a-0d860f76e3ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382824} {:answer/id #uuid "44e3ce89-0fb0-5271-8574-d381668934c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f861ee0-c10a-55b7-b421-ea16180f7604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382821} :answer-field/id #uuid "221ebaf4-de8e-55b9-8386-ea7500ef7627" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382820}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0a7d3d7-12b8-5fcb-bcca-49538501dfd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-105571" :question/problem "![](images/86/864ce19530f022e52b7c9513c0cebc64e898f6e71ecf1066ab6aad92d6df40bf.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $\\text{div}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F>0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere, the vector field $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "005c8e0e-15a2-595a-8bf4-9ff48deefe1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238082} {:answer/id #uuid "5a77fe81-e649-5e06-8e48-0a04dd7aa841" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238835} {:answer/id #uuid "b9ee5d37-f557-5170-896e-a0186821d84e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239673} {:answer/id #uuid "ed516192-424f-5a7a-a664-5896e5376a49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240142} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e231ae-d734-5b35-81c3-81ff9183634d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed516192-424f-5a7a-a664-5896e5376a49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240142} :answer-field/id #uuid "86eda225-7e4d-5e5f-9ce8-78deff9e5db6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240568}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident 