rs decrease as we move horizontally and vertically towards the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51328c89-8cdd-5ec3-96c7-541a1dd6496d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424206} {:answer/id #uuid "53e20d90-4e62-516b-ae3d-39f75e6f0b26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424207} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ea2773-828b-51a5-b56b-8c1ce5f3d0df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424215} {:answer/id #uuid "c14c9ebe-eb84-5d9c-8dd2-5d0a1d71c120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424217} {:answer/id #uuid "cd47d87e-fdba-55d2-b443-02e11d1f6d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1ea2773-828b-51a5-b56b-8c1ce5f3d0df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424215} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8def5ae-54bc-5cde-af27-af1ce38db335" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424224}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5777673f-cba1-5775-ae01-f18d4531f239" :question/math-academy-id "q-131195" :question/problem "![](images/39/39dfec02c787141d8a728381f4093dfe9cbf3fdbf996177ba6f24d973015dc5f.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components increase as we move vertically away from the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f86bf7-5c17-5cb6-8c9a-25e3a30c5a86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424197} {:answer/id #uuid "26003085-8363-5ee2-8473-69f3ba602654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424201} {:answer/id #uuid "4452f92a-63c8-5376-8e98-947775e29e69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424202} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd6eba8-c859-5a8d-b522-f2c3b0ee5a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424204} {:answer/id #uuid "8aff9788-bb7e-5bb2-9074-85e6460dae3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18f86bf7-5c17-5cb6-8c9a-25e3a30c5a86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424197} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fd542c0-4d3b-584d-9d7b-3c0d0b5750ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424223}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9278ec55-4908-5c1c-8a74-640611473537" :question/math-academy-id "q-131196" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4bf8de7e8719545b3d8abf5dc9fb4bc0c5f71034dba7186b7d9822d9e8be044d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement III is true, whereas statements I and II are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $x$-components decrease as we move horizontally towards the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Divergence"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133881 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133697 :question/id #uuid "cb99a3e1-9cd1-5335-b20c-01ad598d14bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-8929" :question/problem "![](images/71/71c58bf1b0fe4259ac50633a457f6a76639c0a0c188b66cce5e0fc3c7e5b0329.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0$\n\n- $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0$\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational\n\n- $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F$ points in the positive $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $\\mathbf F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $\\mathbf F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq  \\mathbf 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F =  \\mathbf 0.$\n\n![](images/86/86aee33cdfa96ac0a61468cc9657e791e399f31c024cdbc3a932193dc897d534.png)\n\nIf $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0$ everywhere in $\\mathbb R^3$, we say that $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, whereas statement II is true. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0.$\n\n- Statements III is false. Since $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0,$ the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is not irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point upward. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ points upward, in the direction of increasing $z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a9cd3e2-8671-58a7-af9d-9b142858986a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238430} {:answer/id #uuid "380f12b9-a21d-5ea0-ac39-89cfdac360c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238539} {:answer/id #uuid "41316ca6-75ad-569d-b0fd-213755940c6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238623} {:answer/id #uuid "776223b9-bded-5959-85bb-2f4bc8533978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239085} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2f0eb8-8278-57bc-a99e-77d279985477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "380f12b9-a21d-5ea0-ac39-89cfdac360c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238539} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9a1229f-1636-5482-97b6-beea488e2f98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240739}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a72f589-a1bb-57d0-9bf2-23704868e98f" :question/math-academy-id "q-105092" :question/problem "![](images/38/38a7ae83a66a2c1ff6a012b9405c6621b0c0a162018065549e75a1d08bc04d44.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](images/59/594106633e422d24b9fea113d24fb6367fdef5ac52724931860e4163e33ce992.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statements III is true. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "984afe37-2fa9-58b4-8e69-c02e0263af15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130535} {:answer/id #uuid "a6e142d6-154b-55cc-8a86-91ca44984e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130536} {:answer/id #uuid "c0149d09-0041-507b-9723-b5bde29dd903" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130537} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c124d7-8cc0-57ff-98f6-d85fe98a935e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130538} {:answer/id #uuid "22ab8d9e-65f2-5914-bcca-b8904b2ff74e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130539}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8058c5ae-a794-5103-96a5-0833641fcaa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130534}] :question/id #uuid "4556fe88-939e-5892-9221-116e661c0c0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105103" :question/problem "![](images/04/04c6eb44298b639520994f01ef505ff40eba58307a1a40e7a48dfb0ebaae5d7e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x, y, z)$, shown above. Given that $F(x, y, z)$ is independent of $z$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $curlF = 0$\n2. $curlF \\ne 0$\n3. $curlF$ points in the negative $z$-direction.\n4. $F$ is irrotational"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241106 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e57f851-cf3f-55d4-b6be-245074197a0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238179} {:answer/id #uuid "7df0fc8d-f369-5515-8dd0-ace7e700bb71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239149} {:answer/id #uuid "c5cda894-ceda-51a8-8065-1c4f43584d91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239797} {:answer/id #uuid "d6768cb9-87b9-5e7b-a6a9-12f7fcc8710a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239929} {:answer/id #uuid "f78ce625-4ae6-52d5-838c-e9371ad41674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6768cb9-87b9-5e7b-a6a9-12f7fcc8710a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239929} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6affe32-6ffe-5e4a-9737-d7047243c7ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240648}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d7d9f8f8-5aac-59da-a7d3-267484c84687" :question/math-academy-id "q-105667" :question/problem "![](images/77/773ff9d880deed92f8034d6949cfee85edbcdf735c8e125f1ec5762137fadb9c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](images/4e/4e344a0a1f6b0c535c5ba08ee8c87914cf5e7009600f45732023720048b4c493.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statements III is true. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational.\n\n- Statements IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241108 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "176e6b67-52d3-5d93-b66b-069cdee86520" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238257} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf364cd-83cb-56e2-a131-b83254f7db14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238452} {:answer/id #uuid "49657e38-b3e1-5210-81e9-349ff603d055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238693} {:answer/id #uuid "5fdb155e-0fc6-579e-9b06-82febc84665a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238892} {:answer/id #uuid "6c31e118-15dd-5a06-b2df-5dc22bae417c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49657e38-b3e1-5210-81e9-349ff603d055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238693} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcea847e-0d5c-5ba8-a276-bb66df6bad46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240774}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c2154fc-c947-5ba9-b169-f98f4fea25a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-105669" :question/problem "![](images/24/2414de6e2129d4fceaae304d16f9be0c91a545e5a8544b2033f353ddb2e581b9.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the positive $z$-direction.\n- $F$ is irrotational" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](images/86/86aee33cdfa96ac0a61468cc9657e791e399f31c024cdbc3a932193dc897d534.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point away from the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points away from the screen, in the direction of increasing $z.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "122217e5-263b-5c2f-a1a6-72cd3fe09ec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238207} {:answer/id #uuid "1d352e77-efb7-5062-9266-9d12f3ebc8ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238301} {:answer/id #uuid "27dca027-745d-5b20-9ded-c32694cd03a6" :answer/type {:db/ident