irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate clockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (clockwise), our thumb will point into the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points into from the screen, in the direction of decreasing $z.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266080 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "063a878d-5e77-56b6-a73e-b63e1d49fa39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263056} {:answer/id #uuid "16b6734b-5505-5646-a97a-a8ee2a8ba3a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263213} {:answer/id #uuid "1740b55b-0519-5f5d-a189-ef0c5a09b842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263221} {:answer/id #uuid "19bcf365-570c-583d-9efd-87145dc67749" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263246} {:answer/id #uuid "5b043794-cc9f-5600-8de5-98b1c61c7162" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "063a878d-5e77-56b6-a73e-b63e1d49fa39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263056} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cec731f-7c57-5058-87bf-7218fda9c7e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265431}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f317d036-e0ac-5288-a673-4543ead19ce4" :question/math-academy-id "q-131366" :question/problem "![](images/78/781a4e33b8b55762b2dcb7575d08315b5842f83d6d78a5136a1c353eefd3069d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](images/97/979b63f21e56924bd7880f9487cf829089e38cf14d287e70196c7a661e41f97f.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382833 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb9ffee0-1c14-5d56-a130-16c686e072e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II,  III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382835} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e2b688-dc4a-51df-b31f-74da402d876d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382836} {:answer/id #uuid "6381a206-d5fa-5c9b-8720-ec49b61ff117" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382837} {:answer/id #uuid "588732a1-fc6e-5c4a-9436-1d13cd58e9bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382838} {:answer/id #uuid "00779e67-ad05-535b-b943-40c3609f48db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6e2b688-dc4a-51df-b31f-74da402d876d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382836} :answer-field/id #uuid "02c6328b-2940-51d9-8cf9-3b5e06746101" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382834}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b44da0aa-9add-572b-86e2-310e134b6864" :question/math-academy-id "q-105664" :question/problem "![](images/56/5643e87bfb5dfb5c29750f48abffe2443bf656901d74a47a0aa39bddfd0c1590.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the positive $z$-direction.\n- $F$ is irrotational" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has a curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](images/86/86aee33cdfa96ac0a61468cc9657e791e399f31c024cdbc3a932193dc897d534.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate clockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point away from the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points away from the screen, in the direction of increasing $z.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4aa1e0b-9e7d-59ae-9899-d11044920abc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382842} {:answer/id #uuid "d6df9fc5-1104-52ed-a184-4d2bd32896ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382843} {:answer/id #uuid "9759f40e-1f08-5303-89ee-4ca26be85dea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382844} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd8b4f3-d0c1-52aa-842b-692f07bfed4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382845} {:answer/id #uuid "8103b8e5-449a-53a9-b201-653ba1001eac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcd8b4f3-d0c1-52aa-842b-692f07bfed4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382845} :answer-field/id #uuid "4300c88e-843e-54be-b2b4-1d2f12e7f48a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382841}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "73b73866-28ec-57e7-b02e-d1bd053c3918" :question/math-academy-id "q-105693" :question/problem "![](images/10/10a9960200e87e1b7b8a38789d5f70f29e48ad1f0a6e2913713da90c532eca6e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is not irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](images/59/594106633e422d24b9fea113d24fb6367fdef5ac52724931860e4163e33ce992.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statements III and IV are false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational. And, since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1524a5a3-8447-5f82-93e3-7232aaf62e6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409592} {:answer/id #uuid "35e57ca5-117c-5fdf-a50f-85738cb2bbce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409593} {:answer/id #uuid "417d24bb-a5fd-5fe7-8f35-35582aaa48b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409594} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd98a2b-6add-530c-b212-292b094d2a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409597} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3bf220-d29b-58bf-82fa-15d03e8e1406" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1524a5a3-8447-5f82-93e3-7232aaf62e6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409592} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1ff360a-7344-55e5-9541-0cbd28d2ee59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409608}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "656969c6-847a-533c-a9b8-d526f82e1ab3" :question/math-academy-id "q-105694" :question/problem "![](images/17/17637e938a25e91b9a03921dbbe0f22c8ea760f09cfed9b4a76d07bd5e3b9301.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction.\n- $F$ is irrotational" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](images/f3/f385add60afac473a5734f8c1ed57c6575bada03c9c16b24fed73b200c38e2c5.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate clockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (clockwise), our thumb will point into the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points into the screen, in the direction of decreasing $z.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133882 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133698 :question/id #uuid "69c1029c-777f-5a16-8ff5-9f8f0c9a48b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9071" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0), (1,0)$ and $(0,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\cos{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+x\\sin{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y}= \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\,\\textrm d A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a78a98750e3203760d27daaebd42852198538935e2c3777a0b8a8c85d1bb0901.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)= \\cos{y}$ and $Q(x,y)= x\\sin{y}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y) = 2\\sin{y}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf385144-2f48-5874-8c0d-dec31d8fc6e3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38de2abc-a7b1-5d3a-8957-d1ab9e5fb1ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241691} {:answer/id #uuid "40e4b86c-bcbb-5404-8559-d21f662ac62b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}({y}^{3}+{x}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241762} {:answer/id #uuid "657bd0c3-147f-5fc7-9596-1342db9f58cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242091} {:answer/id #uuid "c432a38f-4f2b-5dfa-8915-d2bb0d8669d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242903} {:answer/id #uuid "e27db473-126a-5337-8f68-decd55d0a82a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(y-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38de2abc-a7b1-5d3a-8957-d1ab9e5fb1ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241691} :answer-field/id #uuid "63363597-99c8-5060-88d1-1a0669413b41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243630}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00a6ca58-bb1e-5472-9379-ee67967bcfed" :question/math-academy-id "q-108588" :question/problem "Consider the square $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(0,1),(1,0)$ and $(1,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+{x}^{2}\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/aa/aa60dd35a920d166f7cc3238a366bde93bd2c33e1b39b0a38313ab46a8de2d8b.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2(x-y)\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2(x-y).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134075 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17ebf303-f63a-56d2-84bc-bbaa1d0e9a51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241386} {:answer/id #uuid "795e8e88-c522-5db6-90b6-0eabc5c96150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242249} {:answer/id #uuid "8dbff0ef-e453-5de9-b0b2-d423d9e27b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242427} {:answer/id #uuid "cde97e8e-d758-5755-a2d7-f375bdd195e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242994} {:answer/id #uuid "d9edd3d8-8ce5-5d63-b21b-565e48377530" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9edd3d8-8ce5-5d63-b21b-565e48377530" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243121} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b1f949a-6640-5db4-b58d-c6d2f237a658" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243516}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b395a92-9fb2-5e47-8972-f8a867f5011a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109161" :question/problem "Consider the path $C,$ in the plane, as the boundary of the region enclosed by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y\\,dx+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/c4/c44b320dd7ba2824ae0024a3d77d0c6cd6e06fa5889e23879f3d6f464c3a80df.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y\\,dx+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y-(-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y+2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ee90334-9a31-5d11-96f1-a862c794ad22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241596} {:answer/id #uuid "9b9964f5-61c7-5dad-85cd-0db18b40db50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242540} {:answer/id #uuid "c4759efa-de8a-5792-950e-a484236ceef0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242909} {:answer/id #uuid "c4be2895-6c99-5609-9a60-3156b1346f57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242911} {:answer/id #uuid "cebd7864-29a0-5f2c-ae2f-532f9334815b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ee90334-9a31-5d11-96f1-a862c794ad22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241596} :answer-field/id #uuid "b037f3a6-dbaa-5eb8-92d4-902e6c52b98d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243754}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "538aef16-d53a-586e-bc6f-7e8857a93b64" :question/math-academy-id "q-109111" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+3xy\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/0d/0dfe132146c4095954d6b628b055317c975fed6ec429b9ad48b68f4015d6c033.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=3xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+xy\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=y.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4392ecc9-1c7a-5314-823d-2a3728f88f79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241789} {:answer/id #uuid "55eae171-7790-51de-a772-4f26b25540d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241953} {:answer/id #uuid "bab79b1a-7547-5a12-bf33-fdf7b3fa5652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242818} {:answer/id #uuid "c716c726-6e2d-518f-81e9-c48a409738ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242941} {:answer/id #uuid "f292d218-8ce3-5932-9bf6-b4fd5ac83454" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4392ecc9-1c7a-5314-823d-2a3728f88f79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241789} :answer-field/id #uuid "78e21ec4-8926-5116-b2a6-997d0e821869" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243672}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c38c694f-327f-51b0-bb98-98b827fdfa0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109112" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),(2,1)$ and $(0,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/8d/8d57fb7f6b8e2e822a11f11a0267994c449c37b43df167a8d48ab8036854b8dd.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{x}^{2})]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)={x}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34fb3361-d343-5c61-a959-37c900d6bd89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2x){e}^{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241643} {:answer/id #uuid "9ab1a6c3-68d4-5733-8fe4-2d2f237787c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242533} {:answer/id #uuid "bfcafdda-b6d1-5664-99bd-4f07a22445bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-y){e}^{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242857} {:answer/id #uuid "e7caa5e6-91d0-52e8-ad17-08e071cfb41a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(xy-{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243231} {:answer/id #uuid "e871b6fc-462a-5b9e-9b8d-327730b6487f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ab1a6c3-68d4-5733-8fe4-2d2f237787c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242533} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1c47a83-1efa-5d30-b13b-23152d473cad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243856}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "809d1894-c8be-5baf-9170-eb9dccb8466c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109114" :question/problem "Consider the quadrilateral $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(1,0),(1,1)$ and $(0,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,dx+2xy{e}^{xy}\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/b7/b7fad52bdf54c0870ad4c4939a314f5657e390b589faf76e60a8d8ef5d926039.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}$ and $Q(x,y)=2xy{e}^{xy}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=(2y+x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=(2y+2x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,dx+2xy{e}^{xy}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[(2y+2x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}-(2y+x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y+2x{y}^{2}-2y-x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23d8c066-7867-5eac-9b9c-1696e4efd675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241492} {:answer/id #uuid "36db06cb-9213-5e4b-8216-0dea5618ec38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241663} {:answer/id #uuid "53428bc9-6dd9-5fe3-9c0f-9fdc6c63f8f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241925} {:answer/id #uuid "c127b9bd-45f4-56f2-b3b5-d08e61034189" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242873} {:answer/id #uuid "f50b50e7-49d5-52e4-8ac0-dfb07852b27a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f50b50e7-49d5-52e4-8ac0-dfb07852b27a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243345} :answer-field/id #uuid "c72e9f53-402d-51aa-8ec3-8bb61e794fdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243791}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "791b6409-d8b7-533d-ab9f-9fd6eeb81e0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109130" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,2),(1,0)$ and $(2,0),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})\\,dx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/8e/8e3efc3f9c58fb0f9a84144e97a300b6ab2a4011d47ee0d2bae1edc8a3f721cd.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})\\,dx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5190752e-a1c8-5dd8-b834-158e58825739" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5190752e-a1c8-5dd8-b834-158e58825739" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362541} :answer-field/id #uuid "02bbd186-1d4e-5461-bc79-ce35c2890910" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362540}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "231df40f-8e86-536b-af50-43d6f14cbda6" :question/math-academy-id "q-169203" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation of $F$ along the border $C$ of the plot $D,$ shown in the diagram above.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Circulation $\\,={∮}_{C}^{}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={∬}_{D}\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{d}A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}{{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Scalar curl of }F}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}{{(3x{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{P}\\,dx+{{(-{y}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{Q}\\,dy\n$$\n\nIn part 2, we found that the scalar curl of $F$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-6xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}-6xy\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43fe0455-2f42-5377-bc8e-ce440159c478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415794} {:answer/id #uuid "48f5574c-5c8a-571f-a5ec-a59c2f65571f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415795} {:answer/id #uuid "547db7e0-1aff-5b66-8afe-be02f76901b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415798} {:answer/id #uuid "67711d7b-91ec-5a44-89a3-3e9703cd9cc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415805} {:answer/id #uuid "7c5507aa-5a07-5e59-afae-c1b249eef1f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48f5574c-5c8a-571f-a5ec-a59c2f65571f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415795} :answer-field/id #uuid "8258478b-1136-5626-9c52-3604e6dc2cb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415850}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "25b5bd80-ef51-5481-b2fb-7d3f90359e46" :question/math-academy-id "q-109196" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,1),(0,-1),(1,-1)$ and $(1,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3e81cfacc47dfa241a5fea64de84e375a58a6d86ceab82c86303763d5e41fe5.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-(-2y)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ca40ef0-e014-586e-b378-13e06e23ad16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415800} {:answer/id #uuid "8f26a8d9-946b-54f3-9dd0-a27e7daa977e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415815} {:answer/id #uuid "b1502ceb-1419-5afd-b3f7-e9e6c6df5787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y{cos}^{2}⁡(2xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415822} {:answer/id #uuid "c264dd4e-c055-5e7a-bc5a-8d050645e7d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415827} {:answer/id #uuid "d468b014-e971-5635-97c8-eac3fe909b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x-y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c264dd4e-c055-5e7a-bc5a-8d050645e7d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415827} :answer-field/id #uuid "edcf7bc8-f757-5f28-b389-f3fbae1ad417" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415858}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4af8cfd2-bdac-5f91-b83d-a441b5fa79ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-109199" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(0,-1)$ and $(1,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)\\,dx+2{sin}^{2}⁡(xy)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f4/f4bb6636c104cf17bcd4b25037a3490ba30b7c1380b152c33504d9015903bdca.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)$ and $Q(x,y)=2{sin}^{2}⁡xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)=2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)\\,dx+2{sin}^{2}⁡(xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)-(-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415865 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1373e9bd-bdc4-5ee5-9dd4-483158ca8fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415779} {:answer/id #uuid "4388dab3-b038-5c1c-837a-428bf31f6759" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415793} {:answer/id #uuid "6b66bdb8-a403-5ed8-a631-d9248e4a923d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{e}^{-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415807} {:answer/id #uuid "6d060811-a883-579d-9212-b1daa25ef195" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1+{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}){e}^{-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415808} {:answer/id #uuid "c01a8e02-f4d6-5df0-9c30-c6f58e29d206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1373e9bd-bdc4-5ee5-9dd4-483158ca8fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415779} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbfb1dc9-ac7f-533d-9966-b616b6bc9a03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415859}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "581c6229-8cb3-5361-87bc-faf26b91b942" :question/math-academy-id "q-109129" :question/problem "Consider the path $C,$ in the plane, as the boundary of the region enclosed by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the $x$-axis, for $y\\ge 0,$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}x{e}^{-{y}^{2}}\\,dx+{x}^{2}y{e}^{-{y}^{2}}\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/a0/a0d50e8f2faf679e6dee1471c8fa1ca2be4194796e606fc3e89372f0fefe8cfc.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{e}^{-{y}^{2}}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}y{e}^{-{y}^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{e}^{-{y}^{2}}\\,dx+{x}^{2}y{e}^{-{y}^{2}}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}-(-2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}})]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}+2xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=4xy{e}^{-{y}^{2}}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24b36ec1-7764-5048-a4d1-c122f6559c8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415783} {:answer/id #uuid "29edb23d-8a02-50c3-b830-5aa8eaaa3f21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415786} {:answer/id #uuid "388b01e6-6a24-58b4-b0fe-57572ce8b425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415788} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8fc7d9-f9fe-58d9-ada0-460b3e833c02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415789} {:answer/id #uuid "f9106e33-884e-5c11-8c6d-9aed7d907a02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "388b01e6-6a24-58b4-b0fe-57572ce8b425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415788} :answer-field/id #uuid "daa742fd-2059-58a9-8c17-15a7e5c8ca78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415857}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "631300c6-4369-57db-a4e4-4ed4d7169680" :question/math-academy-id "q-109120" :question/problem "Consider the path $C,$ in the plane, as the boundary of the region enclosed by the circle ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1$ and the $y$-axis, for $x\\ge 0,$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}2{y}^{3/2}\\,dx+(x+2x\\sqrt{y})\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/93/93cb529eecb888a110eaad8f55ec458b3710f5a37b497aeed38fa32621178625.png)\n\n \n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2{y}^{3/2}$ and $Q(x,y)=x+2x\\sqrt{y}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3\\sqrt{y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1+2\\sqrt{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}2{y}^{3/2}\\,dx+(x+2x\\sqrt{y})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+2\\sqrt{y}-3\\sqrt{y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-\\sqrt{y})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=1-\\sqrt{y}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415871 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1557707f-89c6-567b-bb30-f8c64368ff64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415780} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf53b6c-fab4-56c1-9e1c-7866457b5b5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415790} {:answer/id #uuid "681da06e-fe7e-503f-ae8f-8cbde680fd97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415806} {:answer/id #uuid "73c8af1d-00e0-5e51-be53-dabd553965d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}-x{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415809} {:answer/id #uuid "af98450c-dfab-596e-b54e-440b639e4538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1557707f-89c6-567b-bb30-f8c64368ff64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415780} :answer-field/id #uuid "b75894da-7bf9-54c7-ab7b-ac317b6f0581" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415854}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dfcce989-6529-57a3-8788-c6f5ffffe36a" :question/math-academy-id "q-108830" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(2,0)$ and $(0,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dx+({x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2b/2bec8a0243d2ea9e983cab4c07719ff4a5d309960039ccdd77e621fd15ffd7ed.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}y+y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dx+({x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133883 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133699 :question/id #uuid "2109910f-fc87-5e56-b2f6-12132b9f6073" :question/math-academy-id "e-9072" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C} 4x^2y \\, \\mathrm{d}x+ 2y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y},$ where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(1,2)$ and $(0,2),$ traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/a8/a87f696b1eb42588ab69800b72d821bf47b35676fc19dcd4fe2927bbf36ba3f8.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)= 4x^2y$ and $Q(x,y)= 2y^2.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) : 0 \\leq x \\leq 1,\\, 2x \\leq y \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}4{x}^{2}y\\,dx+2{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(0-4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2x}^{2}(-4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[y]_{2x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(2-2x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(1-x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}\\,dx \\\\  & =-8{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-8(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{12} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11d3f9e1-dad0-51d4-86a9-e0c49465cc3c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a48ca0e-93ed-5174-bab6-4c899fe31648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241997} {:answer/id #uuid "81c43c9e-66cb-55e7-98de-48e2ff68d8cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242317} {:answer/id #uuid "822c3964-a365-5ee7-be32-2b3f5876cb91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{32}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242321} {:answer/id #uuid "a81cda8b-cfe4-5e44-8bea-ed48a8910f53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242656} {:answer/id #uuid "c3bb5dcb-f2aa-5a8d-a14d-49c9a860d41c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3bb5dcb-f2aa-5a8d-a14d-49c9a860d41c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242899} :answer-field/id #uuid "14ae7574-9b24-5483-829f-b98eb44bc08b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243471}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4e3c164e-33ad-5643-8072-cdedeec68994" :question/math-academy-id "q-109147" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+({e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(2,2)$ and $(-2,2),$ traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7ca4b6fe3a5a16c4004c75b0e4524cc3b42550cb2413d5b8e1c21a1d53cee902.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-y\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+({e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{-y}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3})dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{3}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{[{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7924d408-b0a6-57f4-9535-29236f3d016b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415810} {:answer/id #uuid "950ad376-e54c-57af-bfa1-45e819b52db4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi ({\\pi }^{2}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415816} {:answer/id #uuid "ab82bc31-acab-5197-b9ab-1bdf4558c405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi ({\\pi }^{2}-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415820} {:answer/id #uuid "c5551b46-21d7-59b0-b72c-4198c2a76d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415830} {:answer/id #uuid "d84a9c83-a85a-5496-be1f-846504032a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "950ad376-e54c-57af-bfa1-45e819b52db4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi ({\\pi }^{2}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415816} :answer-field/id #uuid "b60e1b91-b85b-56e9-90f2-353a69264f24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415853}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4381a978-8315-576b-b33a-7ce6b2ca6e4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-41810" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,{x}^{2}-2y⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the square $0\\le x\\le \\pi ,0\\le y\\le \\pi ,$ traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/4f/4f75ee554c61dc799273c2c731d608766d27987937d0058df6ee1e0290d8b060.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+({x}^{2}-2y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}-2y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\pi ,0\\le y\\le \\pi \\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+({x}^{2}-2y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{[2xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]}_{0}^{\\pi }\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\pi x+2)\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\pi {x}^{2}+2x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{3}+2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi ({\\pi }^{2}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244233 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f857c3f-c4d2-5de0-90f5-abf6baaedfa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241458} {:answer/id #uuid "aab8db49-5b5c-570c-9315-cb19a52f8b78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242672} {:answer/id #uuid "d615277f-623e-5e6d-b251-99bf9abe8109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243076} {:answer/id #uuid "e23d1dd7-1f28-56e1-9512-2ecdb8ec1f35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243188} {:answer/id #uuid "ec87942b-fdaa-51d9-accf-3436cee15850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec87942b-fdaa-51d9-accf-3436cee15850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243271} :answer-field/id #uuid "d34cac99-ce8a-559e-a442-6a5f120b6860" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243807}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5f6efefc-16a2-55cb-8b63-0d3253a6815c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109115" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=(3xy-1)\\,i+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,j$ and $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=2x$ and $y={x}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/5f/5f34ac74599937e0ae181126ff9a3da5b29dfe213614a1f23ca10c7062f22f7d.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(3xy-1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3xy-1$ and $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3xy-1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6x-3x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}3x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x[y]_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x(2x-{x}^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}-3{x}^{3})\\,dx \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2(2)^{3}-\\frac{3(2)^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =16-12 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244238 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10dcdef2-642f-55f7-9283-d43907943103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241325} {:answer/id #uuid "54f5783e-24b1-54eb-9c6b-ddf3188e156d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241945} {:answer/id #uuid "de04e406-42a4-5fa6-81c3-f494f888fd31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243155} {:answer/id #uuid "eda54df8-a592-522d-b635-ae2da97bc185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243282} {:answer/id #uuid "edfb2d72-14da-5380-8ba7-43bcec8043f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de04e406-42a4-5fa6-81c3-f494f888fd31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243155} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c1e9f02-a030-5d47-b85b-e2de392e93a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243679}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d2d8f7a5-f5ff-573c-8706-8f2ae5d3ecba" :question/math-academy-id "q-109151" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{x}-{y}^{2},{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the square with vertices at $(-1,-1),$ $(1,-1),$ $(1,1)$ and $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/57/574172dda3031b9bb44dbcfb46265453ae543a75bccf8db24e93625e7154bba9.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({e}^{y}+{x}^{2})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{x}-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{y}+{x}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({e}^{y}+{x}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2xy+{y}^{2}]}_{-1}^{1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2x(1)+(1)^{2}-2x(-1)-(-1)^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}4x\\,dx \\\\  & =2{[{x}^{2}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2[(1)^{2}-(-1)^{2}] \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c3ed13a-1aaf-537e-ad51-fca84471105d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241716} {:answer/id #uuid "40e316c5-a282-5acd-80de-6e530f80656c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241761} {:answer/id #uuid "488aaa87-2228-585a-a9ef-8ec1c4e65922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241839} {:answer/id #uuid "81b8abe5-bd3a-5851-8109-3a0b8505d1f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242316} {:answer/id #uuid "90e17c15-a524-5d11-8dd9-16e39337a7ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c3ed13a-1aaf-537e-ad51-fca84471105d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241716} :answer-field/id #uuid "2637cc62-8f36-5554-86dd-89b757f3c3f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243507}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "13e80aba-bf74-59e1-8677-06e92124ae97" :question/math-academy-id "q-109160" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}(2y-x)\\,dx+(5x-y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=x$ and $y={x}^{2}-x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/e7/e788bbcbda4cc715c6989b7de9d08d60e3b0d178eeb9432a342a4c86c4c01b86.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2y-x$ and $Q(x,y)=5x-y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}-x\\le y\\le x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2y-x)\\,dx+(5x-y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5-2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}-x}^{x}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}[y]_{{x}^{2}-x}^{x}\\,dx \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & =3{[{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3(4-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06018ec3-eeb1-582c-9045-74896f54fbce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{81}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241232} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb0cba9-41d0-58fa-ac43-9d0d6348605b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242570} {:answer/id #uuid "aec24a0e-77a6-5f7d-bf26-bf988d8624c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242703} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f58ec7-1d48-577a-a783-f801a669183c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243099} {:answer/id #uuid "eda11e16-3812-5727-9763-9ffb41a27ea6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06018ec3-eeb1-582c-9045-74896f54fbce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{81}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241232} :answer-field/id #uuid "215f201b-1149-55e4-a109-5b440565fb18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243501}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d29c3a3a-1bb9-50bf-8673-d429ca766812" :question/math-academy-id "q-109164" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}2xy\\,dx+{y}^{2}\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}$ and $y=\\sqrt{3x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/eb/eb0b120cc4d63c8dab18c8acb37f5835e99db746584262f6bb33e8ca99b6a16d.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2xy$ and $Q(x,y)={y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{3x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}2xy\\,dx+{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(0-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}/3}^{\\sqrt{3x}}(-2x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2x)[y]_{{x}^{2}/3}^{\\sqrt{3x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2x)(\\sqrt{3x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})dx \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\sqrt{3}{x}^{3/2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})dx \\\\  & =-2{[\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{3x}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{12}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{2(3)^{2}\\sqrt{3(3)}}{5}-\\frac{(3)^{4}}{12}) \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{54}{5}-\\frac{27}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{81}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3085d7de-40fa-57f6-a2ed-934cac75c3ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{27}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241613} {:answer/id #uuid "53c19e60-39f0-59ab-83fb-c81560577c2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{64}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241930} {:answer/id #uuid "575f8117-55cc-5d5d-a960-2240c1431e26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241973} {:answer/id #uuid "62e9d241-31b4-57e7-8bb2-435dfd18433b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242068} {:answer/id #uuid "f3465b0d-4022-519a-a8c5-5e9237e0a9cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3465b0d-4022-519a-a8c5-5e9237e0a9cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243336} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aac6df8-0c7c-51ef-9bef-7baf99240a0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243619}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7203c090-0409-569d-a6e2-3e62f7801a90" :question/math-academy-id "q-109166" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨2x+{y}^{2},{x}^{2}+2y⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=3.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/fa/fafd281fc63fb276c9759fd84f722096d494aa3e3fe9441f43aa6ecd1f9dfda9.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x+{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+2y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}/3}(2x-2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[2xy-{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{{x}^{2}/3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{9})dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{45}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)^{4}}{6}-\\frac{(3)^{5}}{45} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}-\\frac{27}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326911 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c5474dd-5be4-50d8-bebe-7eb5eb66bf7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324460} {:answer/id #uuid "44ecd8ea-bdce-5d1f-a1a0-626d659605b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324527} {:answer/id #uuid "d8cd2f91-5b3e-58ed-b480-0e9af6226381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325857} {:answer/id #uuid "de55e393-f59f-5979-bf5b-51218edda1ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325912} {:answer/id #uuid "f5723cad-795b-55bb-8ab0-563ed1f0d4a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c5474dd-5be4-50d8-bebe-7eb5eb66bf7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324460} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c9e60ff-5c2c-5bd5-a03b-c43d94b21994" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326376}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "63bc1464-04bd-5198-8418-9e8745d1335c" :question/math-academy-id "q-41811" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{3}+2{y}^{2},{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(2,0)$ and $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/73/73bf9b973e50c540a873f4f1a44d936474e4c49d7041a330902508a840645009.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{3}+2{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):y\\le x\\le 2-y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}-4xy]}_{y}^{2-y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(2-y)^{2}-4(2-y)y-{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{2}-12y+4)\\,dy \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{2}-3y+1)\\,dy \\\\  & =4{[\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}+y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{3}{2}+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415863 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2973f614-b513-5cf6-8c17-61bf7293f111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415785} {:answer/id #uuid "3e7a1a9b-014d-571b-8c35-beddd81764b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415791} {:answer/id #uuid "614e19a5-0156-5aa6-92f4-46816789f3b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415801} {:answer/id #uuid "c3cc7632-9b26-5a7b-bf16-9202ce5e54a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415828} {:answer/id #uuid "f51732c9-6bbf-50cf-aa38-918f942e3f4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "614e19a5-0156-5aa6-92f4-46816789f3b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415801} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6016823-928e-5a70-98df-762771d654fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415852}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4383616d-c261-5116-9385-9c929fedb95e" :question/math-academy-id "q-109157" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}(2x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dx+(x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the trapezoid with vertices at $(0,1),$ $(0,-5),$ $(4,-1)$ and $(4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/ac/acd7ae0cc90007b9f710f242ab1f0498b2439cdbb8951df388b9d8d0588d553f.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(2x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dx+(x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =1+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 4,\\,x-5\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dx+(x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{x-5}^{1}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[y]_{x-5}^{1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(6-x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={[6x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =24-\\frac{16}{2} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415866 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bd1f5ef-c9ed-5994-83b0-1b7bfde99771" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415799} {:answer/id #uuid "64f04a8e-60e7-5cb7-93ee-ee43e5f4bcee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415803} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0a07cf-1f8b-5b69-8fec-824801c47fec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415814} {:answer/id #uuid "f37da209-7b24-5d10-b640-c3d7580c8d8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415840} {:answer/id #uuid "fd217b62-f697-5f47-89d1-aa9d6c12c868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64f04a8e-60e7-5cb7-93ee-ee43e5f4bcee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415803} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3408749-b613-5708-9ee5-7482588129d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415856}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5bd170e0-be10-5a08-9034-fb1371f7f685" :question/math-academy-id "q-41475" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨6{x}^{2}-xy+11,{y}^{2}+1⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(2,-1)$ and $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/00/007634ca34871f05cde9d44a58fcf24b1e5fd3b8c337c68f2025fb8724eef819.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(6{x}^{2}-xy+11)\\,dx+({y}^{2}+1)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6{x}^{2}-xy+11$ and $Q(x,y)={y}^{2}+1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,-\\frac{x}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{x}{2}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(6{x}^{2}-xy+11)\\,dx+({y}^{2}+1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[0-(-x)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-x/2}^{x/2}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x[y]_{-x/2}^{x/2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x(x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "008b0a85-ca8c-5ca9-bac7-3b09c846bdfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415776} {:answer/id #uuid "1752e0da-3afa-549b-a738-e3248a7caa51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415781} {:answer/id #uuid "8a4d1306-f937-5ce3-9c78-97d29184dca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415813} {:answer/id #uuid "a73d50e9-baec-56fe-b31f-f9d4aaf5eb2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415819} {:answer/id #uuid "cac62874-d5b4-593b-8361-008219384519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{31}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a4d1306-f937-5ce3-9c78-97d29184dca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415813} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9adf5ad-146a-5b8c-a73a-531cc3071103" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415855}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6fbdc55e-ae46-5858-9840-dcfa0a36807f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109149" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}xy\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}+y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the rectangle with vertices at $(-1,0),$ $(3,0),$ $(3,2)$ and $(-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/39/3954846460076fa1ef1e5826d32bde9145e7350cb2324072b001d8c6ff41cc14.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=xy$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}xy\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}+y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4x-x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}3x[y]_{0}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}3x(2)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}6x\\,dx \\\\  & =3[{x}^{2}]_{-1}^{3} \\\\  & =3[9-1] \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415869 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bb0f3ad-fee3-562b-9e95-4b5539d42793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415787} {:answer/id #uuid "61c02992-1335-5c6e-a197-06ab323b3dc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415802} {:answer/id #uuid "b224effd-33ce-5fb0-9db8-2bd01771711f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415823} {:answer/id #uuid "b4dfbed0-cc00-5564-812f-23a1d3638249" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415824} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e38dea-a3e8-5c30-a757-acb922de2e15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3e38dea-a3e8-5c30-a757-acb922de2e15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415829} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dc55032-e087-5612-84b6-a80c771dff9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415849}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8c2f5a35-b3df-565f-bebb-8a3475dfd0d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-109144" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}(2xy-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2x)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(-1,0),$ $(1,0)$ and $(-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/cb/cb85e51584f0c41fc19210a5ce56289baa652aff3046d5d88c034d187b7e45dd.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2xy-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+2x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2xy-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2-2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(2+2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2y+{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1-x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2(1-x)+(1-x)^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}-4x+3)\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-2{x}^{2}+3x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}-2+3]-[-\\frac{1}{3}-2-3] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals Using Green's Theorem"}]}
